INT. HOSPITAL ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A steel box rising slowly. A gurney crowds the car. On it lies the PATIENT, an old man, pale, an oxygen mask over his mouth.

NURSE PRISCILLA ADEBAYO-KANE (30s, scrubs, hair in a bun) squeezes a bag valve. A DOCTOR (40s, tired, a stethoscope around his neck) watches the monitor.

DOCTOR
Eight more floors.

PRISCILLA
He's holding, Dr. Marsh.

The elevator stops. The doors don't open. The lights flicker.

MARSH
No.

PRISCILLA
It's the generator.

MARSH
How long?

PRISCILLA
Ten minutes, maybe.

MARSH
He doesn't have ten minutes.

The doors part a crack. An axe handle wedges into the gap. The doors grind apart. In the opening stands a figure in a yellow turnout coat: CAPTAIN LUCIANA MOREAU-BANERJEE (40s, helmet, soot on her cheek).

LUCIANA
Fire department. Everybody out the top hatch or I cut you out.

MARSH
We have a patient.

LUCIANA
Then he goes first.

Priscilla keeps squeezing the bag. Marsh braces the gurney. The captain's helmet lamp hits the patient's face and, quietly, she nods.
